Public debt sustainability has traditionally been assessed through quantitative indicators such as debt-to-GDP ratios, fiscal balances, and debt servicing capacity. However, recent literature increasingly emphasizes the importance of policy credibility as a critical determinant of sovereign debt sustainability. This paper examines the relationship between sovereign debt and credibility through the business case of Suriname, a small open and commodity-dependent economy that experienced a severe macroeconomic and debt crisis during the period 2020-2022.&nbsp;
<div>
 Drawing upon theories of time inconsistency, sovereign risk, debt sustainability, and fiscal dominance, the study develops a conceptual framework linking debt accumulation, policy credibility, market confidence, borrowing costs, and economic performance. Using Suriname's recent experience of debt distress, sovereign default, debt restructuring, and IMF-supported reforms, the paper analyzes how credibility influences debt dynamics beyond traditional fiscal indicators.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The findings suggest that debt sustainability is not solely determined by the level of public debt but also by the credibility of the institutions responsible for managing it. The erosion of fiscal and monetary credibility contributed to higher sovereign risk premiums, loss of market access, exchange rate instability, and increased financing constraints. Conversely, debt restructuring combined with credible fiscal adjustment, strengthened monetary governance, and enhanced transparency can facilitate the restoration of market confidence and improve long-term sustainability prospects.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The Suriname case demonstrates that credibility functions as a strategic macro-financial asset that shapes both sovereign financing conditions and private sector investment behavior. The paper concludes that effective debt management in small open economies requires an integrated framework based on fiscal discipline, transparent debt governance, central bank independence, domestic capital market development, and institutional policy coordination. Ultimately, while debt levels matter, credibility remains the decisive factor underpinning sustainable public finances and economic resilience.
</div>

Stress has emerged as a significant global challenge with implications extending beyond individual health and wellbeing. While stress is commonly examined as a psychological and medical concern, growing evidence suggests that its consequences also affect economic performance at organizational and national levels. The present study investigates the relationship between stress and Gross Domestic Product (GDP) by examining how different forms of stress—including workplace stress, financial stress, health-related stress, educational stress, entrepreneurial stress, and broader socioeconomic stress—influence productivity, labor market participation, healthcare expenditure, human capital development, innovation, and economic growth.
<div>
 A systematic literature review methodology was adopted to synthesize evidence published between 2010 and 2022. Academic databases, peer-reviewed journal articles, international organizational reports, and policy publications were reviewed to identify empirical and theoretical studies examining the economic consequences of stress. Particular attention was given to literature published by the World Health Organization (WHO), International Labor Organization (ILO), Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD), World Bank, and leading academic journals in public health, economics, psychology, and organizational behavior.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The findings indicate that stress affects GDP through multiple interconnected pathways. At the individual level, chronic stress impairs cognitive functioning, decision-making ability, physical health, and psychological wellbeing. At the organizational level, stress contributes to absenteeism, presenteeism, employee turnover, burnout, reduced&nbsp;innovation, and lower productivity. At the national level, stress increases healthcare expenditures, reduces labor force participation, weakens human capital development, and constrains economic growth. The review further reveals that financial stress, educational stress, and health-related stress often generate economic consequences comparable to, or greater than, those associated with workplace stress alone.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The study concludes that stress should be regarded not only as a public health concern but also as an economic challenge with measurable implications for GDP and national development. Investments in stress prevention, mental health promotion, financial wellbeing, educational support systems, and organizational wellbeing programs may generate substantial economic returns by enhancing productivity and strengthening economic resilience. Future policy initiatives should integrate psychological wellbeing into broader economic development strategies to promote sustainable and inclusive growth. &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
</div>
